colorado state representative tom  sullivan ran for office after his son  was killed in the mass shooting at a  movie theater in aurora colorado  27-year-old alex sullivan was one of 12  people who died that day  in 2012. state representative tom  sullivan joins us now  representative sullivan thank you so  much for joining us i'm telling you  it is the stories that get to me and i  remember reading about your son i  remember hearing about his story  these people went to the grocery store  your son went to the movie theater  nothing  nothing can prepare you for the these  kind of moments  so can you help us understand what the  families are going through now  you've been there absolutely i mean to  hear that i mean there  she said it was 12 hours before they got  um the notification and and that was  um you know what it took for us you know  our shooting happened at 12 38 in the  morning we didn't get notification until  8 10 p.m um so you know that was my  concern you know  how how are they going to be they talked  about the room you know we sat in the  exact same room with  all of the families you know in  different you know sections  um getting briefings trying to keep it  together  um you know it's yeah it's it's just  yeah it's just it's helpful it's awful  tom what helped you most and  and what is your message for the  families now who are in so much pain  well one of the things that i i try to  remember  to to let them know is is that like you  know right now you need  the people that you trust the people  that you love that's who needs to be  around you  um that's who you need to reach out to  there are good intention  people around but you know when the  cameras leave and the lights are dimmed  again  it's just going to be you and them again  and and that's who um  needs to um remember and and quite  frankly i mean to tell you  it takes a special friend to to walk  into that room that you're setting in  with you know  nine other families waiting for that  word and and you you will  you will truly know who you can count on  someone who would walk into that room  who has no reason  to be in there other than their love for  you  hey representative sullivan i'd like to  talk a bit about  the possible legislative response to  this mayor weaver there of boulder  told us earlier he thought a national  assault weapons ban  would have helped i presume you agree  but when you talk to republican  lawmakers  in your state there in colorado what  kind of response do you get when you  bring up what some consider common sense  gun control legislation  well they're they're they're spreading  fear this this is their their  their thing is to spread fear they they  had record-setting  uh number of firearm transfers here in  the state of colorado  um last year we had over 500 000  of them um you know transfer um  yesterday we had  a uh hearing on uh reporting of lost and  stolen firearms  of which 60 of them already do it  and when we brought that up their first  thing was to say this was  the beginning of registration um they  were comparing us to nazis  for doing something like this and quite  frankly 60 of them  are doing it already and no one's  confiscating their firearms  no one's coming and getting them but  that's what they tell people  and and the you know the floodgates are  opening once again  to go and buy more firearms because  someone is coming to get you well that  is an unfortunate echo  in the aftermath of events like this  there is a tragedy there's a  conversation about  reform efforts and then there is a rush  to go out and buy more firearms  we heard an argument from senator ted  cruz yesterday  that these proposed changes to gun  legislation  wouldn't in fact stop tragedies like the  one we saw on monday  what's your response to that all of  these  collectively all of these types of  things what they do is they save lives  and that's what i'm in the business of  from from the day my son was murdered  to the day i'm not here any longer i'm  doing everything i can  to save lives yeah and it's not there's  no perfect  answer there's no there's no you know  pill that's going to stop this  there's not one law that will stop this  but each  one will help us save lives and i want  to end talking about your son  tom because you couldn't have known on  that day whenever it was that that might  have been your last conversation with  him  how do you keep him close  well i i have his jacket um that i wear  every day when i go down to the state  capitol on  on the real tough days i i wear it um  you know on the floor with me  you know i can see his eyes i can see  that smile yeah  um i i would just tell those people you  know hang on  um to the voice if you've got any um you  know text messages you've got any um  phone messages they left because that's  the first thing you lose  it's it's difficult to try to re you  know hear him anymore  um so um you know and it's the work yes  hang on to the voices steve hartman is a  correspondent here who does one of the  best human interest stories  i think ever and one thing he said after  the las vegas shooting i think he said  we know this song so what do we do we  mourn pray  repeat we mourn pray repeat and i just  look forward to the day where we don't  have to play that song anymore  thank you so much for the work you're  doing thank you  thank you tom sullivan
 

